export const edition1857=m`<p>This is an independent modern-English translation of the complete memoir, including its introduction and all 27 numbered sections, together with all three preliminary papers printed immediately before it. The basis is David R. Wilkins’s German LaTeX transcription, December 1998, corrected April 2000. <a href="source-de.tex">Download the pinned German text</a>, <a href="https://www.maths.tcd.ie/pub/HistMath/People/Riemann/AbelFn/abfnfig.mp">Wilkins’s source diagram definitions</a>, or <a href="source-evidence.json">the section inventory</a>. The memoir itself occupies journal pp. 115–155. The source bundle’s pp. 101–155 includes the three preliminaries, which are separate papers rather than §§1–3 of the memoir. The 1857 offprint and the original journal volume are linked above.</p><p>All source arguments, formulas, tables, author footnotes, and the four topology-example captions are retained in English, with repeated expressions abbreviated by vector and sum notation. Headings, passage divisions, hyperlinks, margin notes, and drawings are editorial. The figures are explanatory reconstructions; they do not reproduce the exact shapes of the source plates. We have not collated every word and formula against the original printing. This is a reading edition, without proof-assistant verification.</p><p>“Polynomial” translates <i>ganze Function</i> where a finite degree is specified; “holomorphic” and “meromorphic” appear in commentary to make the analytic classes precise. “Single-branched” translates <i>einändrig</i>. “The same branching” means functions single-valued on the fixed regular surface, with the possibility of forgetting sheets explicitly retained. Poles and zeros use the local coordinate on that surface, including points above infinity. Periods, cut-bank directions, and Riemann’s theta exponent are not silently replaced by a later normalization.</p><p>The text corrects the crosscut-count slip at the end of preliminary paper II, the finite/infinite slip at the beginning of §8, and the slope denominator in §6. It uses leading asymptotic ratios for the infinitesimal equalities in §7, and degree bounds where cancellations can lower the degree in §5. Section 4’s opposite logarithmic coefficients are normalized consistently with the signs specified in §25. The nonconstant and constant-multiplier restrictions in §27 are explicit and discussed beside the passage. Other overstrong assertions remain visible and are qualified in the margin, notably special pole dimensions, generic parameter and interpolation counts, identically-zero theta pullbacks, and the genus-one failure of division by \(p-1\).</p><p>The written reconstructions identify their analytic existence dependencies. They do not claim to repair every global step of the moduli, vanishing, or theta-representation arguments. In particular, the preliminary minimum-attainment assertion is not treated as an existence proof merely because its integral is nonnegative.</p><p>An independent published English translation by Roger Baker, Charles Christenson, and Henry Orde appears in <i>Collected Papers</i>, Kendrick Press, 2004, chapter VI, pp. 79–134, including the preliminary material. Its credits and contents are verified from publisher records and the publisher’s front matter. It is linked separately and is not reproduced or used as the basis of this translation.</p>`;
